<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jean Chardin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1643">1643</a>–<a href="/years/1713">1713</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Sir John Chardin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jean Chardin was a French jeweller and traveller who made two long journeys to Safavid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> between 1665 and 1677, passing through Mingrelia, Imereti and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> in 1672–1673. His account, published from 1686, is one of the most detailed European descriptions of Safavid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and of the western Georgian principalities. A Huguenot, he settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, where Charles II knighted him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jean-chardin.jpg" alt="Jean Chardin" width="320" height="399" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Attributed to John Michael Wright · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jean_Chardin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Jean Chardin was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 16 November 1643, the son of a wealthy merchant who was a jeweller of the Place Dauphine, and he followed his father's trade. He began working for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">East Indies</a> with an associate, Antoine Raisin, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a> merchant. They travelled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> in early 1666. In the same year the shah, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-ii-of-persia">Abbas II</a>, made Chardin his agent for the purchase of jewels. In late 1667 he visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> with Raisin, returned to Persia in 1669 and arrived in Paris the following year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1671 Chardin published an account of events he had witnessed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, Le Couronnement de Soleiman Troisième. A learned nobleman, Mirza Sefi, a prisoner in his own palace at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>, had instructed him in Persian and helped with the work. Pétis de la Croix and Tavernier criticised it severely, while Ange de la Brosse defended it. In August 1671 he set out for Persia again with the writer and artist Guillaume-Joseph Grelot.</p><p>A quarrel between the grand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> and the French ambassador made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> dangerous for French subjects in 1672, and Chardin escaped across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> in a small vessel. He travelled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodosia">Caffa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>, which he reached in 1673. At Sapias in Samegrelo he was robbed of nearly everything he owned. He stayed in Isfahan for four years, following the court and making journeys from the Caspian to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a>. The shah commissioned him to import the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> press in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, but opposition from Muslim copyists limited it to liturgy for the Christian minority. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in 1677 by way of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-of-good-hope">Cape of Good Hope</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1681 Chardin settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> because of the persecution of Protestants in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Charles II knighted him at Whitehall on 17 November 1681, and he became court jeweller. He was elected a fellow of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society</a> in 1682 and was sent as envoy to Holland in 1684. The first volume of his travels appeared in 1686, and the fuller Voyages followed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> in 1711. The edition of Louis-Mathieu Langlès, published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1811, is considered the standard version.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montesquieu">Montesquieu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau">Rousseau</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a> and Gibbon praised his work. John Emerson wrote that his information on Safavid Persia outranks that of all other Western writers in range, depth, accuracy and judiciousness. Chardin died at Chiswick in 1713 and was buried at Turnham Green. A monument in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a> bears the inscription nomen sibi fecit eundo, meaning that he made a name for himself by travelling.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jean Chardin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>European visitors, including the French traveller <b>Jean Chardin</b>, who passed through western Georgia in the 1670s, described the trade and the poverty that accompanied it.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-imereti">Kingdom of Imereti · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Jean Chardin</b> thought the shahs ruled with an iron fist.</blockquote><a href="/societies/safavid-iran">Social Hierarchy of Safavid Iran</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Chiswick, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1688" aria-label="Paris on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4604 458 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Jean Chardin"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5054 728h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1688">What was here in 1688? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
